},{"kind":"content","content":[{"anchor":"discussion","level":2,"text":"Discussion","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Each round makes the best improving move, the first move (i, j) in row-major order on"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"ties, until no exchange of two tour edges for two lighter ones remains. The first vertex of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"tour","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" stays first. Only "},{"code":"tour.vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is read: the result uses the lightest edge of each"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"pair, as every tour does. nil below three vertices."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"A move’s gain is formed as (d(a, b) + d(c, e)) − (d(a, c) + d(b, e)), and the scan stops","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"when no move gains anything. With floating-point weights, rounding decides which moves","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"gain.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"name":"Precondition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The graph is complete; "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"tour.vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" lists every vertex of the graph"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"exactly once; no weight is NaN or infinite; the sum of any two weights fits in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"W"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}],"style":"note","type":"aside"},{"name":"Complexity","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"O(n²) per improving move.","type":"text"}]}],"style":"note","type":"aside"}]}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/tours\/graphprotocols\/graph\/twoopttour(improving:weight:)"]}],"references":{"doc://Tours/documentation/Tours":{"role":"collection","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/Tours\/documentation\/Tours","abstract":[],"kind":"symbol","url":"\